==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|procede||obsolete}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*per-|id1=before|*ḱyesdʰ-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|proceden}}, from {{der|en|fro|proceder}}, from {{der|en|la|prōcēdō||to go forth, go forward, advance}}, from {{m|la|prō||forth}} + {{m|la|cēdō||to go}}; see [[cede]].

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/pɹəˈsiːd/|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/pɹəˈsid/|/ˈpɹoʊsid/|a=GA}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-proceed.ogg|a=US}}
* {{homophones|en|precede}}
* {{rhymes|en|iːd|s=2}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}} {{tlb|en|intransitive}}

# To move, pass, or go forward or onward; to [[advance]]; to [[carry on]].
#: {{ux|en|to '''proceed''' on a journey}}
# To pass from one point, topic, or stage, to another.
#: {{ux|en|to '''proceed''' with a story or argument}}
#: {{ux|en|Work is '''proceeding''' at a steady gait.}}
#* {{quote-book
|en
|year=1948
|author=Bernward H. Willeke
|title=Imperial Government and Catholic Missions in China During the Years 1784-1785
|url=https://archive.org/details/imperialgovernme0000will/
|location=w:St. Bonaventure, New York
|publisher=Franciscan Institute
|OCLC=2357643
|OL=6061160M
|page=31
|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/imperialgovernme0000will/page/31/
|text=There the missionaries learned that they were to stay for a longer period, and they were lodged in a shed surrounded by rice fields. This was different from what they had expected, because they had been told in Canton that Father Ts’ai had arranged for a place in Hsiang-t’an. They therefore wished to '''proceed''' to Hsiang-t’an, but since that was impossible under the circumstances, they asked Liu Shêng-tuan to be their messenger to Father Liu asking him to come to them.}}
# To come from; to have as its source or origin.
#: {{ux|en|Light '''proceeds''' from the sun.}}
# To go on in an [[orderly]] or regulated manner; to begin and carry on a series of acts or measures; to act [[methodically]].
#* {{RQ:Locke Conduct|section=34|pages=105–106|pageref=105|passage=He that '''proceeds''' upon others Principles in his Enquiry into any Sciences, though he be reſolv’d to examine them and judge of them freely, does yet at leaſt put himſelf on that ſide, and poſt himſelf in a Party which he will not quit ’till he be beaten out; {{...}}}}
# To be transacted; to take place; to occur.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Julius Caesar|act=I|scene=ii|page=111|column=1|passage=And he will (after his ſowre faſhion) tell you<br>What hath '''proceeded''' worthy note to day.}}
# {{lb|en|of a rule}} To be applicable or effective; to be [[valid]].
#* {{RQ:Ayliffe Juris Canonici|chapter=Of Absolution|page=18|passage=For it {{quote-gloss|this rule}} only '''proceeds''' and takes place, when a Perſon cannot of ''common Right'' condemn or bind another by his Sentence; {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|legal}} To begin and carry on a legal [[process]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2005|author=Rodney Stich|title=Disavow: Sage of Betrayal
|passage=“Gentlemen, shall we '''proceed'''?” the judge said. <br>From the beginning, Judge Fong appeared bored at Levine's coaxing remarks.}}
# To take an [[academic]] [[degree]].

====Usage notes====
* When used as a [[catenative verb]], ''proceed'' takes the {{l|en|''to''-infinitive}} (i.e. one says ''proceed to swing'', not ''proceed swing''). See [[Appendix:English catenative verbs]].
* Not to be confused with {{m|en|precede}}.
* Many of the other English verbs ultimately derived from Latin {{m|la|cēdō}} are spelled ending in {{m|en||-cede}}, so the misspelling {{m|en|procede}} is common.

